# Copy to .env (gitignored).
#
# AGENTLINGS_HOME does NOT belong in this file. It says where this file and
# `.agentlings/` live, so it can only be read from the real environment —
# a line here would be read after the question it answers had been settled.
# Unset, both sit at the repository root, which is what a laptop wants.
# Note the precedence while you are here: a name set in the environment beats
# the same name in this file, so a host variable quietly wins over a key
# pasted into Settings at the next restart (D-270).
#
# Any ONE of these enables the real executor:
#
# 1. An Anthropic API key:
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
#
# 2. A long-lived Claude Code token — run `claude setup-token` in any
#    terminal and paste the result here:
# CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN=
#
# 3. Nothing: a fresh `claude` login on this machine is auto-detected
#    (if its stored token is stale, jobs fail with a 401 — use 1 or 2).

# Force an executor regardless of detection: claude | simulated
# AGENTLINGS_EXECUTOR=simulated

# Default model for sessions whose role does not name one. Roles override it
# with `model:` in their frontmatter — cheap models for extraction and
# reading, the expensive one only where reasoning is genuinely hard.
# AGENTLINGS_MODEL=claude-haiku-4-5-20251001

# Optional, read-only. The library reads public repos unauthenticated
# (60 requests/hour, enough for a handful of sources); a token raises that
# to 5000/hour. Sent to api.github.com only, never to raw file hosts.
# GITHUB_TOKEN=

# The "Read a code host" connection needs the same token, and unlike the
# library it is never live without one. Read-only scopes are enough: it can
# list issues, pull requests, commits and files, and cannot open,
# comment, merge or push. Switch it on in Settings once the token is set.

# The "Search the web" connection. Free tier at brave.com/search/api; the key
# is sent to api.search.brave.com only. Without search the crew can read a page
# you name but cannot find one — and a missing capability is substituted rather
# than refused, which measured as one run answering from memory and another
# spending its whole budget driving the browser at a search engine (D-053).
#
# Not Google: Custom Search stopped being a general web search on 2026-01-20,
# and engines made after that date are capped at 50 nominated domains (D-054).
# Switch the connection on in Settings once the key is set.
# BRAVE_API_KEY=

# The "US labour statistics" connection. Free at data.bls.gov/registrationEngine;
# the key is sent to api.bls.gov only, in the request body rather than the URL,
# which is why this has its own door instead of riding the web one (D-187).
#
# Registered is 500 requests a day and up to 50 series in one call. Unregistered
# is 25 a day against a bucket shared by every keyless caller — measured empty
# on the first call of a fresh day, twice, with nothing local having spent one.
# Switch the connection on in Settings once the key is set.
# BLS_REGISTRATION_KEY=

# The "Send Telegram messages" connection. Sends happen only when you approve
# a reviewed outbox — the crew never holds this token and gets no tool from it
# (D-075). Make a bot: message @BotFather in Telegram, send /newbot, and paste
# the token it answers with. A bot can only message people who tapped Start on
# it once; an outbox message's "to" is the numeric chat id. Switch the
# connection on in Settings once the token is set.
# TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=

# The "Send Gmail, as you" connection (D-080). All three are written by the
# Connect flow in Settings — you never need to fill these by hand. They are
# your OWN Google OAuth client (console.cloud.google.com → Desktop app; the
# steps are in Settings) plus the refresh token Google issues after you
# approve on their page. Publish the consent screen to production, or Google
# expires the token every 7 days. Switch the connection on in Settings.
# GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=
# GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=
# GOOGLE_OAUTH_REFRESH_TOKEN=

# The "Send WhatsApp Business messages" connection (D-081). Both are pasted
# in Settings, which validates them together with one real call — the Meta
# walkthrough is on the drawer. Business-initiated sends are pre-approved
# templates from a business number; start with Meta's free test number.
# WHATSAPP_TOKEN=
# WHATSAPP_PHONE_NUMBER_ID=
#
# Optional: your own per-message rate from Meta's rate card (e.g. 0.025 for
# US marketing). Stamped on sends.jsonl as your declared figure — Meta does
# not return prices at send time, and the audit records no guess. Unset
# means sends are recorded without a price.
# WHATSAPP_USD_PER_MESSAGE=

# Optional. Lets the "Use a web browser" connection read pages you are signed
# in to, without this app ever handling a password: you log in once yourself,
# save the session, and point this at the file.
#
#   npx playwright open --save-storage=C:\path\to\browser-state.json https://example.com
#
# Log in in the window that opens, then close it. Keep the file out of git —
# it is a bearer token for every site in it. Unset is fine: the browser simply
# works signed out.
# AGENTLINGS_BROWSER_STATE=

# Wave 0's gate. Set a password and the app asks for it once, then rides an
# HttpOnly cookie — on the API and on the WebSocket, which is the surface that
# mattered: an ungated socket is handed every job in a level and its whole
# event log the moment it opens, measured at 946 KB (D-240).
#
# UNSET MEANS THE GATE IS OFF *on loopback*, and the app behaves exactly as it
# did. That is deliberate: a gate that defaulted to closed would lock you out
# of a server already running, possibly with paid jobs in flight.
#
# On any other interface it is COMPULSORY (D-271): see AGENTLINGS_BIND below.
#
# On loopback this is a lock on a door that is still inside the house. The bind
# is still loopback and it is still `tailscale serve`, NEVER `funnel`
# (D-127, D-175) — setting this is not permission to relax either.
#
# Changing it signs every browser out, because the cookie is signed with it.
# The spawned runner never receives it: sessions are LLMs holding `Bash`.
# AGENTLINGS_PASSWORD=

# Where the server listens. Unset is `127.0.0.1` — this machine and nothing
# else, which is what makes an unset password safe (D-127, D-271).
#
# Set it to anything else — `0.0.0.0` on a host — and AGENTLINGS_PASSWORD stops
# being optional: with no password the server REFUSES TO LISTEN and prints why.
# There is no arrangement of the two that puts an ungated server where anyone
# with the URL can reach it.
# AGENTLINGS_BIND=0.0.0.0

# The port. `PORT` is what a host injects, so a deploy needs no port wiring;
# AGENTLINGS_PORT is what you write when the host guessed wrong, and it wins.
# Either way, unset is 4600.
# AGENTLINGS_PORT=4600
